  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms through the\nweekend. Some could be severe on Sunday. Stronger showers and \nstorms may produce locally heavy rainfall, with the potential \nfor localized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nShortwaves will continue to cross the area, bringing scattered\nshowers this evening throughout the forecast area. Showers may\nbe slow moving with little to no steering flow, and as PWATs\nsit around 1.5 to 2.0 inches, heavy rain and localized flooding\nis possible.\n\nPatchy fog could develop tonight, particularly in any areas\nwhich receive rain this evening to saturate the lowest levels.\n\nA cold front will cross the area late on Sunday, and could bring\nsynoptic level forcing that kicks off some stronger\nthunderstorms. The Storm Prediction Center does have a marginal\noutlook for severe storms along our eastern border, as 30 kts of\nshear could help sustain thunderstorms that develop. The\ngreatest threat out of these storms will be that of continued\nheavy rainfall and gusty winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered rain showers and thunderstorms early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move through the north next week, bringing\na chance for rain showers and some thunderstorms. Thunder\npossible in the north on Monday, area wide Monday night, and in\nthe north on Tuesday. More showers are possible late week.\nLooking at potential for 500-1,000J/kg of surface CAPE, some\nshear maybe 30-40kts, but mid-level lapse rates are weak. Cold\nfront associated with a shortwave moving through on Tuesday\nseems to be weak as well. PWATs generally in the 1.25-1.5 \nrange. Overall, environment does not look too impressive for \nstrong thunderstorms, so could just be some brief isolated \nstorms below severe criteria.",
